WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.036 --> 00:00:02.186
Hi, I'm Ashher Syed with the IBM Cloud team.

00:00:02.326 --> 00:00:04.636
Today, I'm going to discuss serverless, but first,

00:00:04.636 --> 00:00:07.146
let's define what serverless actually means.

00:00:07.846 --> 00:00:11.366
Does it mean that there are no servers involved?

00:00:12.096 --> 00:00:14.026
The answer is no, obviously.

00:00:14.426 --> 00:00:20.186
What it means is that you're not responsible
for managing and provisioning of these servers,

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:25.726
it's sort of outsourced to the cloud provider
whereas you are as a developer focused

00:00:25.726 --> 00:00:28.126
on writing code or business logic.

00:00:28.576 --> 00:00:32.826
And our deployment models have been
evolving over the years, so let me walk you

00:00:32.826 --> 00:00:37.946
through how we have gone to where we are on
the serverless and the journey behind that.

00:00:37.946 --> 00:00:44.536
So, on the Y axis you see increasing business
focus on logic, that means you're concerned more

00:00:44.536 --> 00:00:48.756
about writing code and less about
the underlying infrastructure.

00:00:49.076 --> 00:00:52.806
And on the X axis, you're seeing
decreasing stack implementation.

00:00:52.806 --> 00:00:56.946
What that means is you are in less
control of what goes behind the scenes

00:00:57.136 --> 00:01:00.096
when you deploy your code or write your code.

00:01:00.096 --> 00:01:04.296
So, first we start off at bare metal.

00:01:04.606 --> 00:01:09.376
Bare metal you were managing and
configuring these servers and the environment

00:01:09.956 --> 00:01:11.506
that you want to deploy your code on.

00:01:11.986 --> 00:01:17.196
That means you are responsible for installing
OS, all the patching and everything.

00:01:17.196 --> 00:01:20.366
So, quite time consuming to
figure out that environment.

00:01:20.366 --> 00:01:23.606
Next, we move to virtual machines.

00:01:24.486 --> 00:01:29.766
Resource optimized from the bare metal where
you were handling idle times much better.

00:01:29.766 --> 00:01:34.636
But at the same time, you're still responsible
for setting up your environment; again,

00:01:34.756 --> 00:01:37.316
installing O/S and patching and everything.

00:01:37.986 --> 00:01:40.646
Down the road, we move to containers.

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:46.196
Actually, Docker popularized this idea.

00:01:46.196 --> 00:01:50.316
Essentially what you're doing is you are
packaging your deployment code, application code

00:01:50.646 --> 00:01:55.076
and all of its dependencies into
a single container which could run

00:01:55.076 --> 00:01:57.126
on any underlying infrastructure.

00:01:57.126 --> 00:02:02.006
It simplified a lot of things from the
deployment point of view but at the same time,

00:02:02.006 --> 00:02:06.136
some challenges with containers
could be when you scale up your apps,

00:02:06.136 --> 00:02:08.066
management of containers becomes a challenge.

00:02:08.436 --> 00:02:12.006
At any given time, your application
code is at least running on one server,

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:13.416
so you still have some idle time.

00:02:13.906 --> 00:02:18.756
Then we move to serverless
which is where we are now.

00:02:19.336 --> 00:02:25.076
And in this, like I said earlier, in this
model you are sort of abstracting from all

00:02:25.076 --> 00:02:29.856
of the underlying infrastructure and you're
focused mainly on writing business logic.

00:02:30.226 --> 00:02:32.766
Let's look at how this serverless
actually works.

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:36.386
All of the major cloud providers
have this Functions as a Service.

00:02:37.906 --> 00:02:44.226
This is essentially a compute platform for
serverless where you run your functions --

00:02:44.226 --> 00:02:50.016
functions are, just again, single
unit of deployment of your code,

00:02:50.016 --> 00:02:52.936
you could say it like a file
processing as an example --

00:02:53.326 --> 00:02:56.826
which is run by events which I called by events.

00:02:56.896 --> 00:03:04.536
Events are anything, think of it as a click of
button the user has, and it creates an event

00:03:04.536 --> 00:03:07.096
which calls a function and
then you run your code.

00:03:07.996 --> 00:03:13.016
And in this serverless Function as a Service
environment you have event driven architecture

00:03:13.556 --> 00:03:20.326
which is, again, an ecosystem provided by cloud
provider which has different services running

00:03:20.326 --> 00:03:24.236
like database, IoT depending on
how you want to build your app.

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:28.856
So, those, all of them are emitting
those events and you build your app,

00:03:28.856 --> 00:03:32.636
you look for those events and
program that into your code.

00:03:32.636 --> 00:03:38.676
And again, like any other platform, some of
the drawbacks of serverless would be timeouts,

00:03:38.676 --> 00:03:42.656
you know, these are stateless containers,
[they] spin off for a little bit

00:03:42.656 --> 00:03:44.476
of time and are deleted afterwards.

00:03:44.476 --> 00:03:49.696
So, if your execution code does not
finish in that time, your app could fail.

00:03:50.046 --> 00:03:53.156
And then, you can also run into
some latency issues depending

00:03:53.156 --> 00:03:56.386
on how time sensitive you want your app.

00:03:56.826 --> 00:04:00.526
So, let me illustrate that into an example here.

00:04:00.526 --> 00:04:06.166
So, for example, a user comes here
on a client side and uploads an image

00:04:06.386 --> 00:04:09.746
and press a button to submit,
it creates an event.

00:04:11.436 --> 00:04:16.276
So, an event calls a function or invokes
a function which does the resizing

00:04:16.276 --> 00:04:19.406
of that image and it stores into storage.

00:04:19.496 --> 00:04:27.246
So, this is just a very over simplified
example of how Function as a Service works

00:04:27.246 --> 00:04:30.226
and how you build your apps in that
environment, serverless environment.

00:04:30.226 --> 00:04:33.016
So, let's look at some of the benefits.

00:04:35.456 --> 00:04:38.116
You pay for execution only.

00:04:38.536 --> 00:04:40.886
What that means is there's no idle time.

00:04:40.976 --> 00:04:50.546
So, like in previous cases where you were always
maintaining a capacity or you're provisioning

00:04:50.546 --> 00:04:53.626
for different scenarios that you
always had something running.

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:57.316
Here, when your function is running, that's
the only time you are paying for that

00:04:57.316 --> 00:05:01.596
and its very cost efficient and functions
usually run at 100 milliseconds of times.

00:05:01.706 --> 00:05:05.496
So, auto scalables.

00:05:05.496 --> 00:05:10.396
That's a responsibility taken
by a cloud provider again.

00:05:10.866 --> 00:05:14.496
When you architect your apps, you're
not provisioning for any of that,

00:05:14.496 --> 00:05:17.516
you are less concerned with the resources.

00:05:18.926 --> 00:05:20.126
Faster time to market.

00:05:20.656 --> 00:05:27.096
Again, since you're not responsible for
any of the management and deployment of any

00:05:27.096 --> 00:05:30.246
of the underlying infrastructure,
you can build your apps faster,

00:05:30.246 --> 00:05:33.766
solve customer problems and
bring them to market.

00:05:35.016 --> 00:05:36.056
Polyglot environment.

00:05:36.396 --> 00:05:44.876
So, like others, I mean, Function as a
Service allows you, you can write any language

00:05:44.876 --> 00:05:46.256
and frameworks that you are comfortable with.

00:05:46.766 --> 00:05:53.176
A lot of cloud providers have multiple languages
that they support, so it could be very helpful

00:05:53.176 --> 00:05:56.806
in cases where you are comfortable with
one language, you want to write in that.

00:05:56.806 --> 00:05:59.796
So, that's totally okay in
the serverless environment.

00:05:59.796 --> 00:06:05.066
Last but not the least is all of the
serverless apps are highly available.

00:06:05.626 --> 00:06:13.066
What that means is that cloud provider takes
care of all the fault tolerance and the MZRs --

00:06:13.066 --> 00:06:16.906
Multi Zone Regions -- that they build to make
sure that your app is always up and running.

00:06:17.266 --> 00:06:22.456
And all the services that you're using in event
driven architecture are also fault tolerant,

00:06:22.456 --> 00:06:26.216
so your app overall is always highly available.

00:06:26.216 --> 00:06:28.616
Thank you for watching the video.

00:06:28.616 --> 00:06:31.036
If you have any questions,
please drop us a line below.

